<title>GERMANY: EU winter grain plantings up 3 to 4 pct - Toepfer.</title>
<headline>EU winter grain plantings up 3 to 4 pct - Toepfer.</headline>
<dateline>HAMBURG 1997-01-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>European Union winter grain plantings in 1996-97 are estimated to be three or four percent above those a year earlier, grain trader Alfred C Toepfer said in a monthly market report.</p>
<p>Winter wheat and barley sowings in Scandinavia were down from earlier plans because the 1996 harvest dragged on much longer than anticipated, it said.</p>
<p>But more spring grains might be sown in the region instead.</p>
<p>The EU's 1996 grain crop stands at an estimated 203-205 million tonnes versus 177 million in 1995.</p>
<p>The land set-aside rate for the 1997 harvest in the EU was halved to five percent.</p>
<p>The picture in winter rapeseed was mixed -- plantings in Germany and France were expanded at a rate ranging between five and 10 percent, Toepfer said.</p>
<p>But some Scandinavian winter rape areas were sharply down from last season.</p>
</text>
